    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-running-differs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Why Running Changes the Ranking Completely</a>
          <a href="#criteria" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Four Specs That Actually Decide It</a>
          <a href="#how-we-tested" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Tested</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Six Best Treadmills for Running</a>
          <a href="#tall-runners" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. If You Are Over Six Feet</a>
          <a href="#cushioning" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Cushioning, Shin Splints and Transfer to Road</a>
          <a href="#incline-decline" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Incline, Decline and Why Decline Is Rare</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A walker can be happy on almost any treadmill. A runner cannot, and the reason is
          geometry rather than power: at 8 mph your stride is roughly twice as long as it is at
          3 mph, and the deck either has room for it or it does not. No amount of motor solves
          a short deck.
        </p>

        <div id="why-running-differs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Running Changes the Ranking Completely</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmill roundups are written around a general home user, which in practice
            means someone walking and jogging between 3 and 5 mph. At those speeds the deck is
            never the limiting factor, cushioning barely registers, and the differences between
            machines come down to the console and the price. Rank the same machines for someone
            running 30 miles a week and the order changes almost entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reason is stride length. A 5'10" runner covers roughly 4 feet per stride at an
            easy 6 mph and closer to 5 feet at 9 mph. On a 55-inch deck that leaves inches of
            margin at the back, and the consequence is not an abstract one: you shorten your
            stride to stay off the rear roller, which changes your gait, which is how treadmill
            running earns its reputation for feeling unnatural and for causing calf and Achilles
            complaints that outdoor running does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second factor is frame rigidity under repeated impact. A running footfall puts
            roughly two and a half to three times body weight through the deck, several
            thousand times per session. A frame that flexes under that load transmits the
            movement back into your stride and makes the machine feel unstable at exactly the
            moment you want it to feel planted. This is where weight is a genuine proxy for
            quality — heavy machines run better because mass resists movement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Motor horsepower, which is the specification most people shop on, is the one that
            matters least once you clear a low bar. Any machine rated 3.0 CHP or above will hold
            pace for a runner of ordinary weight. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why the peak-horsepower figure on the box is close to meaningless.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="criteria" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Four Specs That Actually Decide It</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Deck length.</strong> The single most important number for a runner. Sixty
            inches is the working minimum for anyone over about 5'8", and 60 inches is what we
            would treat as non-negotiable for someone over 6 feet or running faster than 8 mph.
            Fifty-five-inch decks are fine for walking and jogging and become a constraint the
            moment you extend. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            works through the stride arithmetic properly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck width.</strong> Twenty inches is standard and adequate. Twenty-two
            inches is noticeably better on long runs, because fatigue makes foot placement
            wander and a wider belt gives you margin for that without a rail strike. It is a
            comfort specification rather than a safety one, but on runs over an hour it stops
            feeling like a luxury.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame mass and rigidity.</strong> Machine weight is the most reliable
            available proxy. Under 200 lbs assembled, expect noticeable movement at pace. Between
            200 and 250 lbs, most runners will be satisfied. Above 250 lbs the machine simply
            does not move, and that is what commercial-grade money buys more than any headline
            specification.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushioning character, not cushioning amount.</strong> Softer is not better.
            An over-cushioned deck absorbs the elastic return your calves and Achilles are meant
            to get back from each footfall, which makes long runs feel like running on sand and
            trains a gait that does not transfer outdoors. What you want is firm with controlled
            give — closer to a good road shoe than to a mattress. Every machine here was assessed
            on that basis rather than on how soft it felt in the first minute.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Tested</h2>
<p>
            Each machine covered a minimum of 60 miles over at least six weeks, with every
            session logged at 7 mph or faster. The protocol included steady runs of 30 to 60
            minutes, interval sessions at 9 to 10 mph, and at least two runs over 70 minutes,
            because the problems that matter to runners — deck feel degrading, belt drift under
            sustained load, motor heat — show up late rather than early.
          </p>
          <p>
            We measured usable deck length rather than quoting the published belt figure, taking
            the distance from the front roller housing to the point where the rear roller becomes
            a hazard. That number is consistently shorter than the marketing figure, sometimes by
            two inches. We also logged belt drift by marking the belt edge and checking alignment
            after every ten miles, and recorded sound pressure beside the deck at a steady 8 mph.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two testers ran every machine: one at 5'7" and 145 lbs, one at 6'2" and 205 lbs. That
            spread matters, because several machines that were entirely adequate for the first
            tester were not for the second, and a single-tester review would have missed it. Our
            full <a href="/treadmill-reviews-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">testing methodology</a>
            covers the standard protocol every machine completes first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
